commit d7e94f7e101889e4101a874ebfa270f338818ed0
Author: aaron <aaron.hsueh@explorers-residence.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 7 07:32:04 2026 +0800

    fix: alpha_r bypass, on the 10 ms anchor
    
    Two changes, both required for the constant to mean anything.
    
    ANCHOR: 16 ms -> 10 ms. alpha_r = 0.95 was authored at e9cb383 (2026-03-20)
    when the default was frame 512 / hop 256, so 16 ms looked like its reference.
    But the default moved to frame 320 / hop 160 thirteen days later (83ced18,
    2026-04-02) and every commit that has touched or validated it since -- 5407e71's
    RES/Kalman C-parity round and the 800-case rounds after it -- kept 0.95 on the
    10 ms grid. The anchor rule is the last grid the value was actually MEASURED
    on, not the one it was first typed on, so the reference is hop=160/16000 and
    the wall-clock TC is 194.96 ms, not 311.93 ms.
    
    BYPASS: the live error-PSD EMA now reads p->alpha_r. It hardcoded 0.95f while
    pbfdkf_init() computed the retimed value into a field nothing consumed --
    identical to the alpha_power bypass fixed in 5232ab6, in the same file.
    
    Unlike alpha_power, this one reaches audio. p->error_psd feeds e2_ref_sum ->
    use_converged -> the leakage rate applied to H_error_per_bin (pbfdkf.c:986,
    filters.py:911), which is the filter's adaptation gain. So this needs its own
    A/B; alpha_power did not, for reasons recorded in its evidence directory.
    
    Effective values, both ports, all four grids:
      8k/256    0.921208    16k/256   0.959796
      16k/512   0.921208    48k/1024  0.946757
    
    Test targets moved with the anchor. alpha_r leaves the 16 ms identity block in
    test_rate_structural (d2) and the Python effective-value suite, and gains the
    opposite assertion: at a 16 ms grid it must now MOVE. That check is what fails
    if the anchor is ever silently reverted to 256/16000, which is the specific
    mistake this commit corrects.
    
    Verification: rate-structural 292, config-validation 160, detectors-parity
    PASS, Python 88.

